1. Newly-dropped token classes / chain-level unification. Empirically
   verified (ad hoc probe test, run and deleted — repo left clean per
   `git status --porcelain`):
   - Normalize("WALMART #1234")=="WALMART", Normalize("WALMART #5678")=="WALMART"
     → both now merge to one chain-level group. This is NOT a new
     inconsistency: Normalize("WALMART 1234") already collapsed to "WALMART"
     under the pre-existing (pre-P9) all-digit-drop rule, so P9 only extends
     already-accepted chain-level grouping to hash-prefixed store numbers,
     matching the spec's stated intent.
   - Package-doc invariant intact: Normalize("ACME COFFEE")=="ACME COFFEE",
     Normalize("ACME COFFEE STORE #12")=="ACME COFFEE STORE", and
     {ACME,COFFEE} still merges into the 3→2-token group (the dropped token
     was never part of the smaller set's anchor).
   - Constructed regression candidate: Normalize("COSTCO #445")=="COSTCO"
     (degenerate 1-token) vs Normalize("COSTCO WHOLESALE #445")=="COSTCO
     WHOLESALE" (2-token) — these now SPLIT (previously merged only because
     the literal "#445" token was retained in both keys, letting the
     2-token smaller key subset-match). Verified this split is NOT
     introduced by P9: Normalize("COSTCO 445") / Normalize("COSTCO
     WHOLESALE 445") (plain space-separated digits) already split under the
     original all-digit-drop rule shipped in P1, long before this task.
     P9 merely extends an already-accepted trade-off (noise-token anchors
     can be lost) to a new token shape; it introduces no new class of
     defect and no test pins the old coincidental-anchor merge as an
     invariant.

2. Degenerate-key inflation: confirmed real (more letterless tokens dropped
   → more 1-token/empty keys → exact-equality-only), but the only concrete
   instance found (COSTCO case above) reduces to the pre-existing P1-era
   all-digit characteristic, not a new hole. No case found where inflation
   causes a wrongful MERGE (the guard still requires exact equality below
   2 tokens); only wrongful non-merge (fragmentation), which is the safer
   failure direction and unchanged in kind from pre-P9 behavior.

3. CHECK group math, verified against real bank data (not synthetic):
